# the second build `npm run verify` makes: the same tree with the four suite
# hooks compiled in (vite build --mode hooks). dist/ stays the deploy artefact,
# which is what smoke.cjs compares against the origin.
dist-test

# Two things this project can produce that must never be committed, neither of
# which the Vite template's `*.local` covers.
#
# Vite auto-loads .env, .env.production and the rest from the project root and
# INLINES every VITE_* value into the bundle — so a secret in one is not merely
# committed, it is published in dist/assets/index-*.js. No such file has ever
# been committed (history scanned), which is exactly when to add the line:
# before the first one exists. .env.example is exempted so a template can be
# checked in on purpose.
#
# src/ reads exactly one VITE_* name, VITE_TEST_HOOKS (App.tsx and lib/credits.ts,
# typed in vite-env.d.ts), and it does NOT come from a .env: vite.config.ts
# `define`s it from the build mode, true for `--mode hooks` and false for the
# plain build vercel.json runs. That is the point — the value that decides
# whether the suite's window hooks exist has no file a maintainer could copy a
# secret into beside it. This block said "nothing in src/ reads import.meta.env
# today", which the commit that added the mode gate made false the same week.
#
# .vercel/project.json is written by `vercel link` and carries the org and
# project ids; the CLI asks for it to be ignored, and the deploy here is on
# Vercel (vercel.json), so a maintainer running it locally is the ordinary case.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.vercel/

